WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.939
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.939 --> 00:00:10.420
Today we get to be a paper historian, and
witness the amazing progress in machine learning

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.030
research together, and learn what is new in
the world of NERFs.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.840
But, first, what is a NERF?

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:25.220
In March of 2020, a paper appeared describing
an incredible technique by the name, Neural

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.150
Radiance Fields, or NERF in short.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:34.699
This work enables us to take a bunch of input
photos and their locations, learn it, and

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:40.699
synthesize new, previously unseen views of
not just the materials in the scene, but the

00:00:40.699 --> 00:00:43.140
entire scene itself.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:49.500
And here, we are talking not only digital
environments, but also, real scenes as well!

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.559
Now that’s quite a value proposition, especially
given that it also supported refractive and

00:00:55.559 --> 00:01:00.600
reflective surfaces as well, these are both
quite a challenge.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.989
However, of course, NERF had its limitations.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:09.970
For instance, in many cases, it had trouble
with scenes with variable lighting conditions

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.140
and lots of occluders.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:19.750
And to my delight, only 5 months later, in
August of 2020, a followup paper appeared

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.320
by the name NERF in the Wild, or NERF-W in
short.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.060
Its speciality was tourist attractions that
a lot of people take photos of, and we then

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.780
have a collection of photos taken during a
different time of the day, and of course,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.590
with a lot of people around.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.380
And, lots of people, of course means, lots
of occlusions.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:49.310
NERF-W improved the original algorithm to
excel more in cases like this.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:56.280
A few months later, on 2020 November 25th,
another followup paper appeared by the name

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.070
Deformable Neural Radiance Fields.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.180
D-NERF.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.070
The goal here was to take a selfie video,
and turn it into a portrait that we can rotate

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.930
around freely.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.979
This is something that the authors call a
nerfie.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:15.969
If we take the original NERF technique to
perform this, we see that it does not do well

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:23.660
at all with moving things and that's where
this new deformable variant really shines.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:29.120
And today’s paper not only has some nice
video results embedded in the front page,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.620
and it offers a new take on this problem and
offers quote “Space-Time View Synthesis

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.840
of Dynamic Scenes”.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.819
Whoa, that is amazing.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:42.689
But what does that mean?

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:45.420
What does this paper really do?

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:51.599
The space-time view synthesis means that we
can record a video of someone doing something.

00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:58.480
Since we are recording movement in time, and
there is also movement in space, or in other

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.129
words, the camera is moving.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:04.180
Both time and space are moving.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:10.209
And what this can do is one, freeze one of
these variables, in other words, pretend as

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:13.409
if the camera didn’t move.

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:20.290
Or, two, pretend as if time didn’t move.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:29.499
Or, three, generate new views of the scene
while movement takes place.

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:37.719
My favorite is that we can pretend to zoom
in and even better, zoom out even if the recorded

00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:45.389
video looked like this, or, we can also make
a really choppy family memory smoother and

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:48.640
much more enjoyable.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.470
So how does this compare to previous methods?

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.779
There are plenty of NERF variants around,
is this really any good?

00:03:56.779 --> 00:03:59.459
Let’s find out together!

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:04.629
This is the original NERF, we already know
about this, and, we are not surprised in the

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.519
slightest that it’s not so great on dynamic
scenes with a lot of movement.

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:16.459
However, what I am surprised by is that all
of these previous techniques are from 2020,

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:19.250
and all of them struggle with these cases.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:24.330
These comparisons are not against some ancient
technology from 1985.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.259
No-no, all of them are from the same year.

00:04:27.259 --> 00:04:32.750
For instance, this previous work is called
Consistent Video Depth Estimation, and it

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.280
is from August 2020.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.330
We showcased it in this series, and marveled
at all of these amazing augmented reality

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.450
applications that it offered.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.660
The snowing example here was one of my favorites.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:52.460
And today’s paper appeared just three months
later, in November 2020.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:57.710
And the authors still took the time and effort
to compare against this work from just three

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.250
months ago.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.540
That is fantastic.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:06.810
As you see, this previous method kind of works
on this dog, but the lack of information in

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.550
some regions is quite apparent.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:16.060
This is still maybe usable, but as soon as
we transition into a more dynamic example,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.400
what do we get?

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.730
Well, pandemonium.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.490
This is true for all previous methods.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:28.569
I cannot imagine that the new method from
just a few months later could deal with this

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:31.509
difficult case…and.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:33.120
Look at that.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.120
So much better!

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:39.409
It is still not perfect, you see that the
we have lost some detail, but witnessing this

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:44.599
kind of progress in just a few months is truly
a sight to behold.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:49.360
It really consistently outperforms all of
these techniques from the same year.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.139
What a time to be alive!

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:57.560
If you, like me, find yourself yearning for
more quantitative comparisons, the numbers

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:04.900
also show that the two variants of the new
proposed technique indeed outpace the competition.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.440
And it can even do one more thing.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.770
Previous video stabilization techniques were
good at taking a shaky input video and creating

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:21.820
a smoother output, however, these results
often came at the cost a great deal of cropping.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:27.060
Not this new work, look at how good it is
at stabilization, and it does not have to

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.330
crop all this data.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:58.319
Praise 

00:06:58.319 --> 00:07:15.370
the papers!

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:24.750
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

